# shellcheck shell=bash
# Immutable inputs for the native macOS reproducible-build pipeline.
#
# These versions were re-derived from the manually-run reference workflow at
# https://github.com/tongvanlinh/macdeploynunchuk/blob/main/.github/workflows/nunchuk-macos_matrix.yml
# (build, sign, and notarize successfully today), per an explicit instruction
# not to trust the previous values in this file -- they came from an
# unverified draft of this pipeline, never exercised in CI. Every version
# below is copied from a command that reference workflow actually runs, not
# invented. Where the reference pins an exact commit (Olm, HWI) that
# verification is kept; where it only pins a tag (qtkeychain) no commit hash
# is added here, since none was available to verify against.

QT_VERSION=6.9.3
QT_MODULES="qtmultimedia qtnetworkauth qtwebengine qtwebchannel qtpositioning qt5compat qtshadertools"

# No exact commit pin: the reference workflow clones this tag with no commit
# verification either.
QTKEYCHAIN_VERSION=0.14.2

# Bumped from the reference workflow's 1.81.0. contrib/libnunchuk/contrib/
# bitcoin/cmake/module/AddBoostIfNeeded.cmake -- a vendored Bitcoin Core
# CMake module this app's configure step always reaches, that this pipeline
# is not permitted to patch -- hard-requires `find_package(Boost 1.73.0
# REQUIRED CONFIG)`, i.e. a Boost install with generated CMake package-config
# files providing the Boost::headers target. That target was only added
# Boost's own installed BoostConfig.cmake starting at 1.82 (confirmed: Boost
# 1.81 does not reliably emit it). Capped below 1.91: Boost 1.91 removed
# support for the pre-C++11 pattern (a struct inheriting from
# boost::multi_index::indexed_by<...>, then passed as the index-list
# argument to multi_index_container) that the same vendored contrib/bitcoin
# tree's txmempool.h/miner.h/txorphanage.cpp/txrequest.cpp use, which this
# pipeline is equally not permitted to patch (see bitcoin/bitcoin#35175 for
# upstream's own fix, which is not an option here). 1.85.0 sits safely inside
# both constraints. This is a deliberate deviation from the reference
# workflow: the reference never explicitly installs or pins a Boost for this
# nested lookup at all, and silently depended on whatever Homebrew boost the
# runner image happened to have preinstalled -- undocumented, unpinned, and
# the reason this broke once GitHub's runner image moved Homebrew's own
# boost to 1.92. This pipeline instead builds its own pinned Boost (below)
# and feeds it to both the app's own BOOST_ROOT-based lookup and this CONFIG-
# mode lookup, rather than relying on brew.
BOOST_VERSION=1.85.0
BOOST_SHA256=be0d91732d5b0cc6fbb275c7939974457e79b54d6f07ce2e3dfdd68bef883b0b

LIBEVENT_VERSION=2.1.12
LIBEVENT_SHA256=92e6de1be9ec176428fd2367677e61ceffc2ee1cb119035037a27d346b0403bb

# Berkeley DB 4.8.30.NC via Bitcoin Core's own installer script, exactly as
# the reference workflow fetches and patches it (macOS no longer ships a
# usable Homebrew berkeley-db@4 formula).
BDB_INSTALLER_COMMIT=d3a352256064d62a3e2e2d054ffa0faecb49dd71
BDB_INSTALLER_SHA256=1fe15c16d418f9c0dd0319e4982af8e4fc2f3e66b554df6a1004ee8732362d19

OLM_VERSION=3.2.16
OLM_COMMIT=7e0c8277032e40308987257b711b38af8d77cc69

HWI_VERSION=3.2.0-displayaddress
HWI_COMMIT=8d035894a35042d5e8b2936faf140e03518bc4db
# Built from source for BOTH architectures via contrib/build_bin.sh, matching
# the reference workflow -- unlike the previous (unverified) draft, this does
# not use a prebuilt upstream x86_64 release asset.

# OpenSSL is NOT downloaded separately: it is the copy already vendored at
# contrib/libnunchuk/contrib/openssl inside the libnunchuk submodule, pinned
# by that submodule's own commit (see contrib/libnunchuk in .gitmodules).
# There is no independent OPENSSL_VERSION/SHA256 here because this pipeline
# does not choose that version -- libnunchuk does, and checking out the
# recorded submodule commit is what pins it.

# Python is installed via pyenv for the HWI build only (both architectures),
# matching the reference workflow's `pyenv install -s 3.9`. Deliberately not
# pinned to an exact patch (e.g. 3.9.23): that exact pin previously broke on
# GitHub-hosted macOS arm64 runners, which have no darwin-arm64 build of any
# 3.9.x patch at all via actions/setup-python, and python.org itself no
# longer publishes a macOS installer for late 3.9.x security releases.
# pyenv instead compiles CPython from source, and "3.9" resolves to whatever
# latest 3.9.x pyenv's current formula knows -- consistent with what the
# reference workflow already relies on.
PYTHON_VERSION=3.9

XCODE_VERSION=16.4
XCODE_BUILD_VERSION=16F6
MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=13.0
